# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, so a Windows clone of
# this repo gets a CRLF working tree unless told otherwise. That isn't
# cosmetic here: tests/test_vm.c's
# test_type_show_example_runs_correctly reads examples/type_show.logo
# off disk and compares the interpreter's output against a literal
# expected string, so the stray \r at each line end makes it fail on
# Windows and only on Windows. Forcing LF in the working tree on every
# platform keeps the one checked-in corpus byte-identical everywhere.
#
# This fixes the *repo*. Source the user supplies is handled separately
# and no longer depends on this: logo_normalize_newlines (src/lexer.h)
# rewrites ingested source to LF at every point it enters the program,
# so a .logo file written in a Windows editor behaves exactly like an
# LF one. The two are complementary rather than redundant -- this keeps
# the checked-in test corpus byte-identical across platforms, which is
# what the test above compares against.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary assets -- never munge these.
*.png binary
*.wav binary

# .lgb is NOT binary despite the extension: bytecode_disassemble writes
# it and bytecode_assemble parses it back as line-oriented text (see
# bytecode.h), so it wants the same LF normalisation as everything else.
*.lgb text eol=lf
*.svg text eol=lf
